A ticket counter, written twice.

```csharp
public struct CountsStruct { public int Open; public int Closed; }
public class CountsClass  { public int Open; public int Closed; }

var a = new CountsStruct { Open = 3, Closed = 1 };
var b = a;            // the whole value is copied
b.Open = 99;
// a.Open is still 3 — b is a separate piece of data

var c = new CountsClass { Open = 3, Closed = 1 };
var d = c;            // only the reference is copied
d.Open = 99;
// c.Open is now 99 — c and d are the same object
```

Now the nullability half, in a project with nullable reference types enabled:

```csharp
int? reopenedCount = null;          // a real nullable value type
int total = reopenedCount + 1;      // will not compile: int? is not int
int safe = (reopenedCount ?? 0) + 1;// fine, and honest about the absent case

string? assignee = ticket.Assignee; // may be null, and the compiler knows
int len = assignee.Length;          // warning: possible null dereference
int ok  = assignee?.Length ?? 0;    // no warning, and no crash

string name = LoadFromJson().Name!; // compiles silently; can still be null at run time
```

The last line is the one to remember. `!` removes the warning, not the risk. If `LoadFromJson` deserialised a payload with a missing `name`, that variable holds null and the crash arrives at the first use, exactly as it did in V1's `as Order` example.

