items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Priority for a support ticket, as a switch expression over patterns.

```csharp
public enum TicketStatus { Open, Waiting, Closed }

public static string Priority(Ticket ticket) => ticket switch
{
    { Status: TicketStatus.Closed }                 => "none",
    { IsVip: true, AgeHours: > 4 }                  => "critical",
    { IsVip: true }                                 => "high",
    { AgeHours: > 48 }                              => "high",
    { Status: TicketStatus.Waiting }                => "low",
    _                                               => "normal",
};
```

Arms are tested top to bottom and the first match wins, so ordering is part of the logic: moving `{ IsVip: true }` above `{ IsVip: true, AgeHours: > 4 }` would make the critical arm unreachable, and the compiler will tell you so.

And the `Try` pattern, for input that is invalid rather than exceptional:

```csharp
if (int.TryParse(request.Limit, out int limit) && limit is > 0 and <= 100)
{
    return Page(limit);
}
return BadRequest("limit must be a whole number between 1 and 100");
```

`int.Parse` would throw on `"abc"`. A user typing `abc` into a query string is not an exceptional condition — it is Tuesday — so the version that returns a boolean is both faster and more honest about what happened.

