Four small differences, each shown against its TypeScript counterpart.

```csharp
// 1. Nominal typing — these are unrelated types.
public record TicketDto(string Id, string Subject);
public record TicketRow(string Id, string Subject);

TicketDto dto = row;          // does not compile, despite identical members
TicketDto ok = new TicketDto(row.Id, row.Subject);  // explicit mapping required

// 2. as performs a real conversion, not an assertion.
object payload = LoadPayload();
Ticket? ticket = payload as Ticket;   // null when it is not a Ticket
if (payload is Ticket t) { Use(t); }  // test and bind in one step

// 3. Equality is decided by the type, not by the operator.
var a = new TicketRow("t-1", "Printer");
var b = new TicketRow("t-1", "Printer");
bool same = a == b;                   // true: records compare by value

public class Plain { public string Id = "t-1"; }
bool alsoSame = new Plain() == new Plain();  // false: reference comparison

// 4. Integer division truncates.
int pct = 7 / 2;        // 3
double real = 7 / 2.0;  // 3.5
```

The first item explains a shape you will see constantly in .NET services: explicit mapping between a database row, a domain object and a response DTO. In TypeScript that mapping is often skipped because the shapes are compatible by definition. In C# the compiler requires it, which costs a few lines and buys a boundary that cannot drift silently.

